# conversations-bisaya-chat.kb - Conversations / Bisaya chat
# Format: concept | intent | definition | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# The Cebuano shelves taught the language: pronouns, question words, the
# grammar of the affixes. What none of them did was cover the messages a
# person actually sends when they are talking to somebody they know, in
# Cebuano, on an ordinary day.
#
# That gap has a specific cost. "Naayo ka na?" is one of the most common
# friendly check-ins in Cebuano, and with only the grammar shelves behind
# it the bank has nothing to say beyond what the words mean - so a person
# who asked how they were gets a vocabulary lecture instead of an answer.
#
# The entries below are grouped by the ROLE a sentence plays in a
# conversation rather than by grammar: check-ins, permission, reassurance,
# complaint, farewell. That is the distinction that matters, because a
# listener who knows a word and a listener who knows what the word is
# doing get very different results out of the same message.
#
unsa na | definition | Unsa na is so what now, and it is the standard way to move a conversation forward after something has been settled or dropped. The na is the same particle that turns a statement into a question elsewhere in the language, so unsa na reads as now what rather than what - it is looking for the next step, not reopening the previous one. Replying by answering the earlier question again is the usual mistake, because it treats the conversation as still being on the old topic. | so what, now what, next step, forward, particle, question, topic, mistake, move on | what does unsa na mean, how to reply to unsa na, is unsa na a question | anong next | unsa na
naa gyud akong problema | definition | Naa gyud akong problema is I really do have a problem, and the gyud is doing the work again. In Cebuano gyud marks something the speaker is confident about, so it turns a hedge into an assertion - the sentence is a claim that the problem is real, not a maybe. The practical consequence is that it is an opening rather than a request for help, and the reliable response is to ask what happened rather than to start solving something they have not described. | problem, gyud, confidence, assert, hedge, real, opening, ask, solve | what does naa gyud akong problema mean, is naa gyud a hedge, how to reply when someone has a problem | may problema ako | naa gyud akong problema
unsa imong g-iya | definition | Unsa imong g-iya is the Cebuano way of asking what someone is up to, and it is the everyday replacement for the English what are you doing applied to ordinary days. The g-iya covers what a person is doing, what they are up to, and - with a slightly different stress - how they are doing, which is why the same sentence can be casual or genuinely concerned. It is the Cebuano counterpart of the Filipino kumain ka na ba as a way of showing you are thinking about someone. | up to, doing, g-iya, what are you doing, casual, concerned, care, everyday, counterpart | what does unsa imong g-iya mean, how to reply to unsa imong g-iya, is it a greeting | ano ang ginagawa mo | unsa imong g-iya
kay nga | definition | Kay nga is the Cebuano discourse marker for as I was saying, and it is the Cebuano equivalent of the Filipino kasi, which the dictionary renders as because but which in practice marks the speaker returning to their own point. It often follows an interruption, and it carries a small note of I had already said that rather than genuine complaint. The reason it is worth knowing is that it is frequent in ordinary speech and translating it as a plain because loses the conversational work it does. | as I was saying, marker, kasi, because, return, point, interruption, complaint, frequency | what does kay nga mean, is kay nga the same as kasi, what does kasi mean in filipino | kasi nga | kay nga
mahal kita | definition | Mahal kita is I love you, and in Cebuano it is a much heavier sentence than the English phrase it translates to. Mahal covers both love and expensive, and the same word buys flowers and says this - which is one reason the sentence is used far less casually in Cebuano than in English or Tagalog. Saying it to a friend can land as a declaration rather than an affection, so the register matters as much as the meaning. | love, mahal, expensive, heavy, casual, declare, affection, register, meaning | what does mahal kita mean, is mahal kita romantic, how to say love in bisaya | mahal kita | mahal ka
adtlaka na ako | definition | Adtlaka na ako is I am leaving or I am off, and the na plus ako marks it as completed rather than ongoing, so the person is already on their way out when they say it. It is the Cebuano counterpart of the Filipino alis na ako, and like that one it is an announcement rather than a question. The useful thing to know is that it is not necessarily a goodbye to the person - it is a statement about movement, and the farewell, kanus-a nimo, is a separate and deliberate choice. | leaving, off, adtlaka, movement, announcement, not goodbye, farewell, separate, choice | what does adtlaka na ako mean, is adtlaka a goodbye, how to say goodbye in bisaya | alis na ako | adtlaka na ako

# 12 entries.

naayo ka na | definition | Naayo ka na is the Cebuano check-in, and it is the Cebuano equivalent of the Filipino kumain ka na ba - a question about your wellbeing that is really an expression of care. The naayo covers being well generally rather than one specific thing, and the na is what turns a statement into a question, so "naayo ka na" is not "are you well" so much as "you are well, right". Replying with an unrelated fact is the common miss. | check, well, fine, care, right, wellbeing, question, reply, common miss | what does naayo ka na mean, how to reply to naayo ka na, is naayo ka na a real question | kumusta ka na | naayo ka na
okay gyud ako | definition | Okay gyud ako is the Cebuano all-clear, and gyud is the load-bearing word rather than a filler - it means genuinely or truly, and it is added precisely because a bare okay would be heard as unconvincing. Like the Tagalog okay lang ako it is used in two directions: as a reply it is sincere, and as a preamble it is I am coping and please do not make a fuss. The gyud leans towards the sincere reading, so using it to dismiss a real problem is noticeable. | okay, all-clear, gyud, genuine, coping, dismiss, preamble, sincere, notice | what does okay gyud ako mean, is okay gyud ako always true, how to reply to okay gyud ako | okay lang ako | okay gyud ako
gikan ako | definition | Gikan ako means I am coming from or I am on my way, and the na makes it the Cebuano equivalent of Tagalog puntahan kita - a statement of movement rather than a request. The word gikan does double duty as "from", which is why the same sentence answers both where are you coming from and where are you, and a reply that reads it the first way misses the point. | coming, from, way, movement, statement, not request, where, double duty, reply | what does gikan ako mean, is gikan ako a request, how to reply to gikan ako, unsa gikan ako | gikan ako na | gikan ako
kaayo unta | definition | Kaayo unta is please, and unlike its literal components it is one of the most formal things a Cebuano speaker can say. Kaayo alone means good or much, and unta marks a hope or wish, so together they mean I hope you will do so - the request is phrased as a wish about the other person rather than as a demand of the speaker. That is why kaayo unta is noticeably more deferential than a plain imperative, and why using it with a close friend can sound oddly formal. | please, formal, deferential, hope, wish, request, imperative, friend, odd | what does kaayo unta mean, is kaayo unta formal, how to say please in bisaya | pakisabi | kaayo unta
kaw makaayo | definition | Kaw makaayo is you are tired, and in Cebuano the phrasing depends on who is being addressed in a way that trips up non-native speakers. Makaayo with kaw is the short, everyday version used with friends and family. The longer forms carry more distance or respect, and using the intimate one to a stranger is a real social error rather than a minor grammar mistake, which is why the choice of pronoun is worth knowing rather than defaulting to one. | tired, tired of, kaw, pronoun, respect, distance, friend, stranger, social error | what does kaw makaayo mean, difference between kaw and ikaw, how to say you are tired in bisaya | pagod ka na ba | kaw makaayo
ayaw nako | definition | Ayaw nako means I do not want to, and the nako is the part that trips people up: it turns a plain statement of dislike into I, on the other hand, decline. The word is one of the most frequently misapplied pieces of Cebuano, because ayaw nako is routinely read as do I not want, when the nako fixes the subject as the speaker. Used correctly it declines an offer, and the sentence that gets it wrong reverses who is refusing. | do not want, decline, nako, subject, speaker, offer, refuse, reverse, misapply | what does ayaw nako mean, is ayaw nako negative, difference between ayaw and dili, ayaw nako meaning | ayaw ko | dili nako gustong
